          "title": "Thermodynamic Refutation of a Super-Carnot Patent Claim",
          "statement": "<p>An inventor files a patent application claiming to have developed a proprietary heat engine that operates between thermal reservoirs at $T_H = 800\\text{ K}$ and $T_C = 300\\text{ K}$, absorbing $Q_H = 1000\\text{ kJ}$ of heat per cycle and delivering $W = 680\\text{ kJ}$ of net mechanical work while exhausting $Q_C = 320\\text{ kJ}$ of heat. Prove via the Second Law of Thermodynamics (both Kelvin-Planck and Clausius formulations) that this device is physically impossible.</p>",
