          "question": "<p>Starting from the definition of Gibbs free energy $G = H - TS$ and the fundamental differential $dG = -S dT + V dP$: (a) derive the Gibbs-Helmholtz equation in both the form $\\left(\\frac{\\partial (G/T)}{\\partial T}\\right)_P = -\\frac{H}{T^2}$ and $\\left(\\frac{\\partial (\\Delta G/T)}{\\partial (1/T)}\\right)_P = \\Delta H$. (b) A chemical reaction has an enthalpy change of $\\Delta H^\\circ = -84.2\\text{ kJ/mol}$ and an entropy change of $\\Delta S^\\circ = -165.0\\text{ J/(mol}\\cdot\\text{K)}$. Calculate $\\Delta G^\\circ$ at $298.15\\text{ K}$ and determine the inversion temperature above which the reaction ceases to be spontaneous.</p>",
