          "answer": "(a) $dT/dP = 2.76\\times 10^{-4}\\text{ K/Pa} = 27.6\\text{ mK/kPa}$. (b) At $70.0\\text{ kPa}$, water boils at $T = 362.9\\text{ K}$ ($89.8^\\circ\\text{C}$), a drop of over $10^\\circ\\text{C}$ below standard sea-level boiling.",
          "question": "<p>At standard atmospheric pressure $P_0 = 1.013\\times 10^5\\text{ Pa}$, water boils at $T_0 = 373.15\\text{ K}$ ($100.0^\\circ\\text{C}$) with a specific latent heat of vaporization of $L_v = 2.257\\times 10^6\\text{ J/kg}$. The specific volume of steam is $v_{\\text{steam}} = 1.673\\text{ m}^3\\text{/kg}$, while the specific volume of liquid water is $v_{\\text{water}} = 1.043\\times 10^{-3\\text{ m}^3\\text{/kg}$. (a) Calculate the rate of change of boiling point with pressure $dT/dP$ at $100^\\circ\\text{C}$. (b) If atmospheric pressure at a high-altitude mountain station is $P = 70.0\\text{ kPa}$, estimate the boiling temperature of water at that station using the integrated Clausius-Clapeyron equation.</p>",
